s ahead for RI governor, but Block is closing the gap", "source": "The Providence Journal", "published": "2026-10-13T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 13.74, "prior_sd": 5.34, "poll_margin": 36.99, "poll_sd": 5.58, "poll_weight": 0.48, "fundamentals": {"margin": 10.35, "lean": 6.0, "environment": 4.35, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.41, "baseline_margin": 24.86, "analyst_adjustment": -6.86, "key_factors": ["Deep-blue Rhode Island state with an 8-point Democratic partisan lean", "All eight expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic", "Final UNH poll shows Foulkes at 55% with the Republican at just 8%", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win at 97-99%"], "rationale": "Foulkes defeated incumbent McKee in the primary and enters the general with a strong polling lead, though the raw margin over the Republican is inflated because independent Ken Block runs second and siphons anti-Democratic votes. With a solidly Democratic state, unanimous expert ratings, and markets near certainty, a high-teens Democratic margin is the reasonable landing point.", "watch": "A late consolidation of independent and Republican voters behind a single challenger could narrow the margin, though not plausibly the outcome.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governorship is open after Dan McKee lost the Democratic primary to Helena Foulkes, a former CVS executive. Foulkes faces Republican Aaron Guckian and a crowded independent field led by Ken Block, who polls show running second but well behind her. The state's 8-point Democratic lean and a favorable recent environment for the party frame the contest.\n\nEvery major poll this fall has shown Foulkes comfortably ahead, with the final University of New Hampshire survey putting her at 55 percent and the Republican at just 8 percent. All eight exper